//! Messages for `ozbox adopt-fleet-key` (English first), looked up by `messages::t`.

pub const EN: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("adopt_key.intro", "Moving {box}'s secrets store to the fleet's KMS key ({key}), in place. What the store holds is kept: its keys are re-wrapped, not replaced. Five things happen, in this order:"),
    ("adopt_key.promise", "  1. {box}'s role is allowed to use that key, only for {box}'s own secrets (the encryption context names the box); the box is marked as moving, which makes `ozbox upgrade` refuse it until it is done.
  2. {box} reads that mark itself (within 15 minutes; this command asks it to look now). It re-wraps a COPY of the store's keys under KMS while its secrets service keeps running (each key is given back through KMS before anything is replaced; this is the slow part, about a second or two per secret, and nothing waits for it), then stops the service, swaps the copy in and starts it again. The service is down for that swap and its start-up: expect a few seconds, plus a little for every secret the service unlocks when it starts (a store with 100 secrets can take a minute or two to be ready). `ozbox env run`, the dashboard's secret reads and the runners wait or retry for that time.
  3. {box} keeps its key file until you commit. This command reads {box}'s state as root through AWS Systems Manager (your AWS sign-in needs the policy the aws-adopt-command module outputs as operator_adopt_policy_json: SendCommand on the fleet's adopt document only, nothing else on the box), and only when the service, the store and the copy of the store on /work are all on KMS does it write the label that makes later upgrades keep the store.
  4. Then {box} destroys its key file and the copy beside the store, and this command waits for that and says what it saw.
  5. If anything fails, {box} puts everything back and nothing is claimed.
The store is kept: no secret is re-entered. If the command stops half way, run it again; `--cancel` asks the box to stop and take its store back, and takes the grant and the mark away only after the box says it has."),
    ("adopt_key.begun", "{box} is marked as moving and its role has the key. Asking it to move its store now."),
    ("adopt_key.resuming", "{box} is already moving to the fleet's KMS key (an earlier run stopped before the box said it was done). Carrying on from there."),
    ("adopt_key.trigger_failed", "The box did not take the request to look at its tags now ({why}); it looks by itself every 15 minutes. Waiting."),
    ("adopt_key.waiting", "{box}'s root has not said its store is on KMS yet. Nothing was claimed: upgrades of {box} stay refused until it does."),
    ("adopt_key.err_cancel_unsure", "{box}'s root did not say it is back on a file key with the cancel recorded, so ozbox will not take the grant back: if the box is part way through the move, the grant is what its store now needs. Nothing was changed on AWS. Run `ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box} --cancel` again in a few minutes (`ozbox admin {box} -- collect-logs` shows what the box did)."),
    ("adopt_key.err_cancel_unreachable", "ozbox could not reach {box}'s root helper through AWS Systems Manager ({why}), so it cannot be sure the box has not moved, and taking the grant back from a box that has would leave its store unopenable. Nothing was changed. (Your AWS sign-in needs the policy the aws-adopt-command module outputs as operator_adopt_policy_json, and the module must have been applied in this region.)"),
    ("adopt_key.err_waiting", "{box}'s root has not said its store is on KMS (the service, the store and the copy on /work all wrapped by the key) yet. The box reads its tags every 15 minutes, a grant on its role can take a few minutes to reach it, and the copy on /work follows within about 30 seconds of the move, so run it again shortly; the same command carries on from where this one stopped. `ozbox admin {box} -- collect-logs` shows what the box did. If you want the box left as it was, `ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box} --cancel` asks the box to stop and take its store back, and removes the grant only after the box says it has."),
    ("adopt_key.done", "{box}'s secrets store now keeps its key in the fleet's KMS key, and what it holds was kept. {box}'s root says the key file and the copy beside the store are gone, and `ozbox upgrade` now keeps the store. Not changed: a key escrowed in your vault before this move is not removed from it, and still opens any copy of the store made before the move (a backup, a snapshot of /work) until you remove it; `ozbox secrets rotate-data-keys` on the box gives every secret a new key for the values stored from now on."),
    ("adopt_key.already", "{box} already keeps its secrets store's key in the fleet's KMS key ({key}); there is nothing to move."),
    ("adopt_key.cancel_intro", "Taking {box} back from moving to the fleet's KMS key. {box}'s root records the cancel first (no move starts or finishes past it) and, if the store already moved, re-wraps it back under the file key from the store as it is now, so nothing written since is lost. Only when {box} says it is back on its file key are the mark and the grant removed. Changes:"),
    ("adopt_key.cancelled", "{box} is back as it was: no tag, no grant. The box keeps a cancel note until you start again (`ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box}` takes it away first). `ozbox upgrade` is allowed again."),
    ("adopt_key.err_aws_only", "Moving a store to the fleet's KMS key is for AWS boxes only: the key service is AWS's. This computer's provider is not AWS."),
    ("adopt_key.err_not_moving", "{box} is not moving to the fleet's KMS key, so there is nothing to cancel."),
    ("adopt_key.err_not_running", "{box} is {state}, so its store cannot be moved: the box does the moving itself. Start it (`ozbox resume {box}`) first. Nothing was changed."),
    ("adopt_key.err_no_key", "No fleet key is configured on this computer, so there is nothing to move {box} to. Set secrets_key_arn and fleet under [aws] (the aws-secrets-key module's outputs). Nothing was changed."),
    ("adopt_key.err_unreachable", "ozbox could not ask {box} where its store's key is ({why}), so it will not change anything about it. Nothing was changed."),
    ("adopt_key.err_older", "{box}'s secrets service is older than this feature and cannot move its own store: it does not say where its key is, or does not say that it can. Nothing was changed. Upgrade {box} first, to an image built from a release that includes `ozbox adopt-fleet-key` (`ozbox upgrade {box}` with a newer image; if the plan says the store would be lost, `--accept-new-secrets-store` starts a new one and the plan lists what to enter again), then run this again: on the upgraded box the move keeps whatever the new store holds, and every upgrade after it keeps the store. Upgrading to an image from an older release gains nothing."),
    ("adopt_key.err_lost", "{box}'s store already cannot be opened (its key was lost with an earlier system disk), so there is nothing to move. Nothing was changed. Run `ozbox admin {box} -- recover-secrets` first to start a new one, then run this again."),
    ("adopt_key.err_not_ready", "{box}'s secrets service is not ready ({why}), so its store cannot be moved now. Nothing was changed."),
    ("adopt_key.err_not_file", "{box}'s store is kept by {kek}, not by a file key, so there is nothing to move to the fleet's KMS key. Nothing was changed."),
    ("adopt_key.done_key_file_stays", "The label is written, so `ozbox upgrade` keeps {box}'s store, but {box} has not yet said that it removed its key file and the copy beside the store. It removes them itself on its next check (within 15 minutes) once the copy of the store on /work is on KMS; until then both stay on its system disk, readable only by root and the secrets user. Nothing needs doing; `ozbox admin {box} -- collect-logs` shows when it did."),
    ("adopt_key.err_root_unreachable", "ozbox could not reach {box}'s root helper through AWS Systems Manager ({why}), so it will not change anything: what the box user says over SSH is never enough to decide this (it can say anything). Your AWS sign-in needs the policy the aws-adopt-command module outputs as operator_adopt_policy_json (SendCommand on the fleet's adopt document only), that module must have been applied in this region so the document exists, and the box needs the Systems Manager agent running (it has it by default). Nothing was changed."),
    ("adopt_key.err_root_older", "{box}'s root helper answered, but not with what this version of ozbox expects: its program is older than this feature. Nothing was changed. Upgrade {box} first (see `ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box}` for what that costs the store), then run this again."),
    ("adopt_key.err_busy", "{box} is moving or cancelling its store right now (or could not take the lock in time). Nothing was changed. Wait a few minutes and run the command again."),
    ("adopt_key.err_marked", "{box} has a cancel recorded, so it will not move. Finish it with `ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box} --cancel`, which takes the mark and the grant away; then start again if you want to."),
    ("adopt_key.err_cancel_failed", "{box}'s root could not take the store back to the file key ({why}). Nothing was changed on AWS: the grant stays, because a store that is on KMS needs it. Fix what it says and run `ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box} --cancel` again."),
    ("adopt_key.err_no_guard", "{box}'s root says the metadata guard is not loaded, so the box user could reach the instance role's credentials, and giving that role the fleet key would let them open this box's KMS-wrapped copy on /work. Nothing was changed. Reinstall it with `ozbox admin {box} -- reinstall-ozboxd`, check `nft list table inet ozbox_metadata` on the box, and run this again."),
    ("adopt_key.err_changed", "{box}'s root says something else now than a moment ago (another run, or another computer, changed the box while this one waited). Nothing was changed on AWS. Look at where {box} stands with `ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box} --dry-run` and run the command you mean again."),
    ("adopt_key.backoff", "{box}'s store is written to more often than it can be re-wrapped, so the box gave up its last tries and waits about {mins} more minutes before it tries again (each try costs the secrets service a short stop, so the waits grow). Nothing was lost."),
    ("adopt_key.cancel_copy_offer", "{box}'s root could not take the store back the usual way ({why}), and says the key service itself REFUSES {box}'s role (an access-denied answer: the grant is gone). The copy of the store made before the move is still on {box}. Putting it back works without KMS, but it LOSES every secret saved on {box} since the move began, and those must be entered again. You are asked twice: the second time you type {box}'s name."),
    ("adopt_key.cancel_copy_not_offered", "The copy of the store made before the move is on {box}, but it is not offered: {box}'s root does not say that the key service refuses its role (it answers, or the check timed out or could not run, which says nothing about the grant), and a blocked network is not a revoked grant. Nothing was lost. Check {box}'s network and egress list, then run `ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box} --cancel` again."),
    ("adopt_key.err_stranded", "{box}'s root says its store is already on the fleet's KMS key and it still holds its key file and the copy made before the move, but the box carries no label for it. Nothing was changed. If the box's role still has the grant, nothing is lost and a person can look at what happened (`ozbox admin {box} -- collect-logs`); if the key service refuses the role, `ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box} --cancel` puts the copy back after reading the cloud's tags and asking you twice."),
    ("adopt_key.cancel_stranded_intro", "{box}'s root says its store is on the fleet's KMS key with its key file and the pre-move copy beside it, the cloud's own tag list shows no label on {box}, and the key service refuses {box}'s role: the grant is gone and the store cannot be opened. The way back is the copy of the store made before the move. It LOSES every secret saved on {box} since the move began, and those must be entered again. You are asked twice; the second time you type {box}'s name, and `--yes` is not that."),
    ("adopt_key.err_stranded_needs_person", "Putting the pre-move copy back loses data, so it needs a person at a terminal: `--yes` is not consent for it. Nothing was changed. Run `ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box} --cancel` without `--yes`."),
    ("adopt_key.err_tags_unread", "ozbox could not read {box}'s tags from the cloud ({why}), so it cannot tell that no label is on it, and what the box says about its own tags is not enough to put a copy back. Nothing was changed."),
    ("adopt_key.err_kms_answers", "{box}'s root says the key service still answers for it, so its store can be opened and nothing is stranded. Nothing was changed. The copy made before the move is not put back: that would lose secrets for nothing."),
    ("adopt_key.err_kms_unchecked", "{box}'s root could not check whether the key service refuses its role (a timeout, a network fault or a blocked egress list says nothing about the grant), so the copy made before the move is not put back. Nothing was changed. Fix {box}'s network and run `ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box} --cancel` again."),
    ("adopt_key.cancelled_stranded", "{box}'s pre-move copy of the store is back under its file key, and a cancel note is on the box, so it will not move until you start again (`ozbox adopt-fleet-key {box}` takes the note away first). Enter again what was saved since the move began. `ozbox upgrade` is allowed again."),
];
